Z("cm2",4,"cm",["An association between an exposure and a disease is not necessarily causal; Bradford Hill's criteria (strength, consistency, specificity, temporality, biological gradient, plausibility, coherence, experiment, analogy) help judge whether an observed association is causal.","Finding smoke near a fire doesn't prove the match started it; Hill's criteria are the checklist a detective uses to decide whether the suspect really caused the crime.",["Types of association: spurious (bias), indirect (confounding), direct/causal — one-to-one or multifactorial.","Bradford Hill criteria (1965): temporal association (cause precedes effect — essential), strength (RR/OR), dose–response (biological gradient), consistency (replication), specificity, biological plausibility, coherence, experimental evidence (removal reduces disease), analogy.","Example: smoking and lung cancer satisfies almost all criteria.","Causation concepts: Koch's postulates (infectious), web of causation, multifactorial causation, sufficient-component cause model (Rothman), necessary vs sufficient causes."],"Hill criteria 'TS-DCS-PCEA': Temporality, Strength, Dose-response, Consistency, Specificity, Plausibility, Coherence, Experiment, Analogy."],
"An association (correlation) is the concurrence of two variables more often than would be expected by chance; statistical association does not necessarily imply causation. Types of association: (1) spurious association — observed association that is not real, resulting from bias (e.g., selection bias in hospital data — Berkson's fallacy; a lower perinatal mortality in home deliveries than hospital deliveries because high-risk cases are referred to hospitals); (2) indirect association — a statistical association between a characteristic of interest and a disease due to the presence of another factor common to both (confounding variable) — e.g., altitude and endemic goitre (iodine deficiency is the real cause); (3) direct (causal) association — (a) one-to-one causal relationship — variable A is both necessary and sufficient to cause B (rare; e.g., some genetic diseases); (b) multifactorial causation — alternative causal factors each can produce the disease independently (e.g., lung cancer from smoking, asbestos, radiation) or several factors act in combination (web of causation for CHD — smoking, hypertension, cholesterol, diabetes, stress, genetics). Additional criteria for judging causality (Sir Austin Bradford Hill, 1965; US Surgeon General's report on smoking 1964): (1) temporal association — the causal attribute must precede the disease; exposure must occur before the onset of disease (only criterion that is absolutely essential); clearest in cohort studies; (2) strength of the association — the greater the relative risk or odds ratio, the more likely the association is causal (e.g., RR of lung cancer in heavy smokers ~20–30); (3) biological gradient (dose–response relationship) — risk of disease increases with increasing dose or duration of exposure (number of cigarettes per day, years of smoking); (4) consistency of the association — the association is observed repeatedly by different investigators, in different places, populations, times and with different study designs; (5) specificity of the association — one cause leads to one effect (weakest criterion; most diseases are multifactorial and exposures have multiple effects — smoking causes many diseases); (6) biological plausibility — the association is consistent with existing knowledge of biology and mechanisms of disease (carcinogens in tobacco smoke); limited by current knowledge; (7) coherence of the association — the association should not conflict with what is known of the natural history and biology of the disease (rising lung cancer incidence parallel to cigarette consumption; histopathological changes); (8) experimental evidence — removal or reduction of exposure leads to reduction in disease (smoking cessation reduces lung cancer risk; animal experiments); the strongest support; (9) analogy — similar associations with related exposures (thalidomide → other drugs in pregnancy). Not all criteria need to be fulfilled; temporality is necessary; the more criteria satisfied, the stronger the case. Other models of causation: Koch's postulates (for infectious diseases — organism present in every case, isolated in pure culture, reproduces disease in susceptible animal, re-isolated; limitations — carriers, viruses, multifactorial diseases), germ theory, epidemiological triad (agent, host, environment), web of causation (MacMahon), wheel model, sufficient-component cause model (Rothman's causal pies — necessary cause present in every sufficient cause), counterfactual approach, Evans' postulates. The smoking–lung cancer association is the classic example fulfilling nearly all criteria.",
[["Types of association",[["Type","Explanation","Example"],["Spurious","Due to bias","Hospital vs home perinatal mortality"],["Indirect","Due to confounder","Altitude and goitre (iodine)"],["Direct one-to-one","Necessary and sufficient","Some genetic disorders"],["Direct multifactorial","Multiple causes","Lung cancer, CHD"]]],
["Bradford Hill criteria",[["Criterion","Meaning"],["Temporality","Cause precedes effect (essential)"],["Strength","High RR/OR"],["Dose–response","Risk rises with dose"],["Consistency","Repeated findings"],["Specificity","One cause → one effect (weak)"],["Plausibility","Biologically sensible"],["Coherence","Fits natural history"],["Experiment","Removal reduces disease"],["Analogy","Similar known associations"]]]],
{c:"Association becomes causation only after excluding chance, bias and confounding and weighing Bradford Hill's criteria — temporality being essential — as exemplified by smoking and lung cancer."});

Z("cm2",5,"cm",["Screening is the search for unrecognised disease or defect in apparently healthy people using rapidly applied tests; the validity of a screening test is expressed by sensitivity (true positive rate), specificity (true negative rate), and predictive values (PPV, NPV), which depend on prevalence.","Sensitivity catches the sick (few false negatives); specificity clears the healthy (few false positives); predictive value tells a person with a result how likely it is to be right.",["Sensitivity = TP/(TP + FN) × 100; specificity = TN/(TN + FP) × 100; PPV = TP/(TP + FP); NPV = TN/(TN + FN); false positive rate = 1 − specificity; false negative rate = 1 − sensitivity.","PPV rises with prevalence (screen high-risk groups); NPV falls with prevalence; sensitivity and specificity are inherent test properties (inverse relationship with cut-off — ROC curve).","Screening vs diagnostic tests: apparently healthy vs sick; groups vs individuals; less accurate, cheaper; positive screen needs diagnostic confirmation.","Criteria (Wilson & Jungner): important health problem, recognisable latent/early stage, natural history understood, suitable acceptable test, effective treatment, facilities available, cost-effective, continuous process; types — mass, high-risk (selective), multiphasic, opportunistic; uses — case detection, control, research, prescriptive screening."],"SnNout: high Sensitivity, Negative rules out; SpPin: high Specificity, Positive rules in."],
"Screening is defined as 'the search for unrecognised disease or defect by means of rapidly applied tests, examinations or other procedures in apparently healthy individuals' (Commission on Chronic Illness, 1951). It is a form of secondary prevention (early diagnosis) and sorts out apparently well persons who probably have disease from those who probably do not; screening tests are not intended to be diagnostic — positive results need diagnostic confirmation and treatment. Lead time: the period between diagnosis by early detection (screening) and diagnosis by usual means (onset of symptoms); advantage of screening depends on the lead time and on availability of effective treatment at the earlier stage. Screening vs diagnostic test: screening — applied to apparently healthy people, to groups, test results arbitrary and final, based on one criterion or cut-off, less accurate, less expensive, not a basis for treatment, initiative from investigator; diagnostic — applied to sick, individuals, diagnosis based on evaluation of a number of signs, symptoms and findings, more accurate, more expensive, basis for treatment, initiative from patient. Uses: (1) case detection (prescriptive screening — for the benefit of the individual, e.g., neonatal screening, cancer screening); (2) control of disease (prospective screening — to protect others, e.g., screening immigrants for TB, blood donors for HIV/HBV/HCV, food handlers); (3) research purposes (natural history of chronic disease); (4) educational opportunities (create public awareness). Types: mass screening (whole population or large group irrespective of risk), high-risk or selective screening (in high-risk groups — most productive, higher yield, higher PPV), multiphasic screening (application of two or more screening tests to a large population at one time), opportunistic (case finding — when patients consult for other reasons). Criteria for screening (Wilson and Jungner, WHO 1968): disease — the condition should be an important health problem (high prevalence or serious consequences), there should be a recognisable latent or early asymptomatic stage, the natural history should be adequately understood, there should be a test that can detect the disease prior to onset of signs and symptoms, facilities should be available for confirmation of diagnosis, there should be an effective treatment, there should be an agreed-on policy concerning whom to treat, there is good evidence that early detection and treatment reduces morbidity and mortality, expected benefits of early detection exceed risks and costs, case-finding should be a continuing process; test — acceptability (simple, safe, rapid, not painful/embarrassing), repeatability (reliability, precision — consistent results on repeated application), validity (accuracy — ability to distinguish between who has the disease and who does not), yield (amount of previously unrecognised disease diagnosed and treated), simplicity, rapidity, ease of administration, cost. Validity of a screening test has two components — sensitivity and specificity — calculated from a 2×2 table comparing screening test results with the true disease status (gold standard diagnostic test): true positives (a) — diseased persons with positive test; false positives (b) — non-diseased with positive test; false negatives (c) — diseased with negative test; true negatives (d) — non-diseased with negative test. Sensitivity (true positive rate) = a/(a + c) × 100 — the ability of a test to identify correctly all those who have the disease; a highly sensitive test has few false negatives — important when missing a case is dangerous (serious disease, effective treatment available, e.g., screening blood for HIV, PKU, cancer); a negative result in a highly sensitive test effectively rules out disease (SnNout). Specificity (true negative rate) = d/(b + d) × 100 — the ability of a test to identify correctly those who do not have the disease; a highly specific test has few false positives — important when false positives lead to harmful/expensive further testing, anxiety, stigma (e.g., confirmation of HIV, cancer surgery); a positive result in a highly specific test rules in disease (SpPin). False negative rate = c/(a + c) = 1 − sensitivity; false positive rate = b/(b + d) = 1 − specificity. Predictive accuracy (from the perspective of the person tested): positive predictive value (PPV) = a/(a + b) × 100 — probability that a person with a positive test truly has the disease; negative predictive value (NPV) = d/(c + d) × 100 — probability that a person with a negative test truly does not have the disease. Predictive values depend on sensitivity, specificity and, critically, prevalence of the disease in the population screened: as prevalence increases, PPV increases and NPV decreases; when prevalence is low (general population), even a highly specific test yields many false positives (low PPV) — hence screening is more efficient in high-risk groups. Percentage of false positives and false negatives; likelihood ratios (LR+ = sensitivity/(1 − specificity); LR− = (1 − sensitivity)/specificity) are independent of prevalence; overall accuracy = (a + d)/total. Cut-off point: for tests with continuous results (blood sugar, BP, PSA), sensitivity and specificity vary inversely with the chosen cut-off — lowering the cut-off increases sensitivity but reduces specificity and vice versa; the receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve (sensitivity vs 1 − specificity) helps select the optimal cut-off (area under curve = overall accuracy). Combining tests: series (sequential) testing — positive only if both positive — increases specificity and PPV (e.g., HIV testing strategies); parallel (simultaneous) testing — positive if either positive — increases sensitivity and NPV. Problems of screening: lead-time bias, length bias (slow-growing tumours more likely detected), overdiagnosis and overtreatment, labelling effect, false reassurance, cost; evaluation of screening programmes ideally by RCTs with mortality endpoints. Examples: cervical cancer (Pap smear, VIA, HPV DNA), breast cancer (mammography, CBE), oral cancer (visual inspection), hypertension, diabetes, neonatal screening (hypothyroidism, PKU, G6PD), antenatal screening (anaemia, syphilis, HIV, Down syndrome), TB symptom screening, HIV in blood donors.",
[["2×2 table",[["Screening test","Disease present","Disease absent","Total"],["Positive","a (true positive)","b (false positive)","a + b"],["Negative","c (false negative)","d (true negative)","c + d"],["Total","a + c","b + d","N"]]],
["Formulae",[["Measure","Formula","Meaning"],["Sensitivity","a/(a+c) × 100","Detects diseased (true positive rate)"],["Specificity","d/(b+d) × 100","Identifies non-diseased (true negative rate)"],["PPV","a/(a+b) × 100","Positive test → has disease"],["NPV","d/(c+d) × 100","Negative test → disease-free"],["False positive rate","b/(b+d) = 1 − specificity","—"],["False negative rate","c/(a+c) = 1 − sensitivity","—"],["LR+","Sensitivity/(1 − specificity)","Independent of prevalence"]]],
["Effect of prevalence",[["Prevalence","PPV","NPV"],["↑","↑","↓"],["↓","↓","↑"],["Sensitivity, specificity","Unchanged","Unchanged"]]],
["Criteria for screening (Wilson & Jungner)",["Important health problem","Recognisable latent/early stage","Natural history understood","Suitable, acceptable test","Effective treatment available","Facilities for diagnosis and treatment","Agreed policy on whom to treat","Cost-effective","Continuous process"]]],
{i:"Screening shifts diagnosis earlier in the natural history of disease; its value depends on test validity, disease prevalence and effective treatment.",x:[["Series vs parallel testing",["Series (both positive needed): ↑ specificity, ↑ PPV","Parallel (either positive): ↑ sensitivity, ↑ NPV","ROC curve selects optimal cut-off"]]],f:"Apparently healthy population → screening test → negative (reassure, rescreen) / positive → diagnostic test → confirmed → treatment\n Lead time = screening diagnosis → usual diagnosis",c:"Screening tests are evaluated by sensitivity and specificity, while predictive values — strongly influenced by prevalence — determine their usefulness in practice; programmes must satisfy Wilson–Jungner criteria and confirm positives diagnostically."});

Z("cm2",6,"cm",["Investigation of an epidemic is a systematic process to confirm the outbreak, identify the cause, source and mode of spread, and implement control measures: verify diagnosis, confirm epidemic, define population at risk, rapid search for cases, data analysis (time, place, person), hypothesis formulation and testing, control measures, and report.","An epidemic investigation is detective work — confirm that a crime (excess cases) has occurred, identify the culprit (agent), trace how it spread, stop it, and write the report.",["Objectives: define magnitude (time, place, person), determine conditions/factors, identify cause, source, mode of transmission, susceptible groups, recommend control and prevent recurrence.","Steps: (1) verification of diagnosis (clinical + laboratory), (2) confirmation of existence of epidemic (cases > expected), (3) defining population at risk (map, denominator), (4) rapid search for all cases (case definition, active surveillance), (5) data analysis — time (epidemic curve: point source vs propagated), place (spot map), person, (6) formulating hypotheses, (7) testing hypotheses (case-control/cohort, attack rates, food-specific attack rates), (8) evaluation of ecological factors, (9) further investigation of population at risk, (10) writing the report.","Control measures (start early): treat cases, isolate, quarantine, chemoprophylaxis, immunisation, safe water/food, vector control, health education, notification.","Epidemic curve: single sharp peak within one incubation period → common-source point exposure; plateau → continuous source; successive waves → propagated (person-to-person)."],"Outbreak steps: 'Verify, Confirm, Define, Search, Analyse, Hypothesise, Test, Control, Report'."],
"An epidemic is 'the occurrence in a community or region of cases of an illness or other health-related event clearly in excess of normal expectancy'; for a disease absent from an area (e.g., cholera in a non-endemic area, polio after eradication), even a single case may constitute an epidemic; outbreak is a localised epidemic. Objectives of epidemic investigation: (1) to define the magnitude of the epidemic outbreak or involvement in terms of time, place and person; (2) to determine the particular conditions and factors responsible for the occurrence of the epidemic; (3) to identify the cause, source(s) of infection and modes of transmission to determine measures necessary to control the epidemic; (4) to make recommendations to prevent recurrence. Steps (Park; CDC): (1) verification of diagnosis — the first step is to verify the diagnosis of reported cases; clinical history and examination of a sample of cases, laboratory confirmation (samples — stool, blood, water, food); a clinical case definition may be used initially; it is not necessary to wait for laboratory results before starting investigation and control; (2) confirmation of the existence of an epidemic — compare the current number of cases with past incidence during the same period (expected frequency/endemic level); determine whether there is a genuine increase (rule out artefacts: changes in reporting, diagnostic criteria, population size, new laboratory tests, seasonal variation); (3) defining the population at risk — (a) obtaining a map of the area (identify natural landmarks, roads, houses, water sources) and conducting a census (to obtain denominator by age and sex), (b) counting the population; (4) rapid search for all cases and their characteristics — (a) medical survey: active case search (house-to-house survey, health facility records, schools, workplaces) using a standard case definition (suspected, probable, confirmed); (b) epidemiological case sheet/questionnaire to collect data — name, age, sex, occupation, address, date of onset, symptoms, history of exposure (food, water, contact, travel), immunisation status, outcome; (c) searching for more cases (contacts, neighbouring areas); (5) data analysis — compile and analyse data by time, place and person: time — prepare an epidemic curve (histogram of number of cases by date/time of onset) — shape suggests type of epidemic: common-source single exposure (point source) — rapid rise and fall, all cases within one incubation period (food poisoning, chemical exposure; allows estimation of time of exposure — median incubation period back from peak); common-source continuous or repeated exposure — prolonged plateau (contaminated water supply); propagated epidemic — gradual rise, successive waves separated by incubation periods (person-to-person — measles, hepatitis A, cholera secondary spread); mixed; place — spot map showing geographical distribution of cases (clustering around a water source, area of residence, workplace) — John Snow's Broad Street pump; person — age, sex, occupation, immunisation status, socioeconomic group, attack rates by subgroups to identify high-risk groups; (6) formulation of hypotheses — based on analysis, regarding possible source of infection, causative agent, mode of spread, and environmental factors enabling it; hypotheses should explain the majority of cases; (7) testing of hypotheses — by analytical studies (case-control comparing exposures of cases and controls, or retrospective cohort in a defined group — e.g., food-specific attack rates among those who ate vs did not eat each item at a function, with relative risk; the food with the highest attack rate among those who ate and lowest among those who did not, and greatest difference, is implicated), laboratory confirmation (culture of food, water, environmental samples, molecular typing); (8) evaluation of ecological factors — sanitation, water supply, food handling, vector breeding, animal reservoirs, weather, population movements, breakdown of health services; (9) further investigation of population at risk — to identify additional susceptible persons, carriers, subclinical infections (serological surveys), effectiveness of control measures; (10) writing the report — background, historical data, methodology, analysis of data, control measures and their effectiveness, recommendations for the future; disseminate to authorities. Control measures (implemented simultaneously as soon as possible, not after investigation is complete): directed against the source/reservoir (treatment of cases, isolation, removal of contaminated food, disinfection of water — chlorination, closure of implicated premises), against transmission (sanitation, food hygiene, vector control, safe water, hand hygiene, safe disposal of excreta), and protecting susceptible hosts (immunisation, chemoprophylaxis — e.g., doxycycline for leptospirosis, rifampicin for meningococcal contacts, health education, nutrition); notification to higher authorities (IDSP — Integrated Disease Surveillance Programme; rapid response teams); surveillance continued until the outbreak ends (two incubation periods without new cases). Integrated Disease Surveillance Programme (IDSP) in India: weekly reporting (S — syndromic, P — presumptive, L — laboratory-confirmed forms), early warning signals, district surveillance units, rapid response teams; IHIP (Integrated Health Information Platform) for real-time reporting.",
[["Steps of epidemic investigation",[["Step","Action"],["1","Verify diagnosis (clinical + lab)"],["2","Confirm existence of epidemic (observed > expected)"],["3","Define population at risk (map, census)"],["4","Rapid search for all cases (case definition, case sheet)"],["5","Data analysis: time (epidemic curve), place (spot map), person"],["6","Formulate hypotheses"],["7","Test hypotheses (case-control/cohort, attack rates, lab)"],["8","Evaluate ecological factors"],["9","Further investigation of population at risk"],["10","Write report and recommendations"]]],
["Epidemic curves",[["Type","Curve","Example"],["Common source, single exposure","Sharp peak within one IP","Food poisoning"],["Common source, continuous","Plateau","Contaminated water supply"],["Propagated","Successive waves","Hepatitis A, measles"]]],
["Control measures",[["Target","Measures"],["Source/reservoir","Treat, isolate, remove contaminated food, disinfect water"],["Transmission","Sanitation, food hygiene, vector control, hand hygiene"],["Susceptible host","Immunisation, chemoprophylaxis, health education"],["System","Notification (IDSP), surveillance until 2 IPs case-free"]]]],
{i:"Prompt, systematic investigation of outbreaks — integrated with IDSP rapid response teams — limits spread and prevents recurrence.",f:"Report of cases → verify diagnosis → confirm epidemic → define population → case search → analyse (time/place/person) → hypothesis → test (attack rates, case-control, lab) → control measures (throughout) → report",c:"Epidemic investigation follows a stepwise approach from verification and confirmation through case search, descriptive analysis, hypothesis testing and ecological evaluation to reporting, with control measures implemented as early as possible."});
